1. 	Aggregation is a strong form of composition, child Objects 
	define parent Objects at a class level meaning they won't
	be out of scope even if the parent object is destroyed.
	
	Composition means Object1 defines an instance of object2 
	at class level, but no other object has access to it. 
	This means the object2 is completely encapsulated
	inside of object1. If object2 is destroyed so is object1.
	
2.	Composition is chosen over inheritance when Objects have a 
	'has-a' relationship. Where as inheritance is chosen when
	objects have a 'is-a' relationship.
	
3.	Encapsulation strengthens composition as it bundles together
	related data and methods. This helps implementation (simplifies)
	and data integrity (hiding an objects internal state, only
	exposing relevant methods). 
	
4. 	Composition makes testing easier by promoting modularity.
	this reduces dependencies, making testing isolated functions
	easier, and quicker;